    "resultsInterpreter": [
      {
        "display": "Michael Tanaka, MD"
      }
    ],
    "conclusion": "1. No acute intracranial hemorrhage or calvarial fracture.\n2. Small left frontal scalp hematoma.\n3. 14 mm extra-axial lesion along the left frontal convexity, most consistent with a meningioma. Consider nonemergent MRI brain with and without contrast.\n4. Mild chronic small vessel ischemic changes.",
    "presentedForm": [
      {
        "contentType": "text/plain",
        "text": "EXAM:\nCT HEAD WITHOUT CONTRAST\n\nCLINICAL INDICATION:\nGround-level fall at home with head strike. On apixaban for atrial fibrillation. Evaluate for intracranial hemorrhage.\n\nTECHNIQUE:\nAxial CT images of the head were obtained without intravenous contrast. Coronal and sagittal reformats were reviewed. CTDIvol: 48 mGy. DLP: 812 mGy-cm.\n\nCOMPARISON:\nNone available.\n\nFINDINGS:\nBrain: No intracranial hemorrhage, mass effect, midline shift, or CT evidence of acute infarct. There is a 14 mm homogeneous, mildly hyperdense extra-axial lesion along the left frontal convexity with a broad dural base and no surrounding edema. Mild periventricular white matter hypoattenuation, consistent with chronic small vessel ischemic change. Age-appropriate volume loss.\nVentricles and cisterns: Ventricles are normal in size for age. Basal cisterns are patent.\nSinuses and mastoids: Mild mucosal thickening of the maxillary sinuses. Mastoid air cells are clear.\nCalvarium and soft tissues: No calvarial fracture. Small left frontal scalp hematoma.\n\nIMPRESSION:\n1. No acute intracranial hemorrhage or calvarial fracture.\n2. Small left frontal scalp hematoma.\n3. 14 mm extra-axial lesion along the left frontal convexity, most consistent with a meningioma. Consider nonemergent MRI brain with and without contrast.\n4. Mild chronic small vessel ischemic changes."
      }
    ]
  },
